1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,133
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. This video is about why AI agents

2
00:00:03,133 --> 00:00:05,267
fail silently, and what actually stops it.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:09,167
Somewhere right now, an AI agent is stuck. Not broken. Not crashed.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,467
Worse — it's still running, still confident, still absolutely sure it's making

5
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:15,433
progress, while it does the exact same thing over and over, burning

6
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:18,933
time, burning money, and reporting "success" the entire way through.

7
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:21,977
This is the part nobody puts in the demo video.

8
00:00:22,443 --> 00:00:25,743
Today we're opening up the four ways agents actually fail — and

9
00:00:25,743 --> 00:00:28,577
then we're going to fix one, live, so you can see exactly

10
00:00:28,577 --> 00:00:29,677
what stops the bleeding.

11
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:33,363
An agent works in a cycle: look at the situation, decide what

12
00:00:33,363 --> 00:00:36,063
to do, take an action, check what happened, repeat. That loop is

13
00:00:36,063 --> 00:00:36,863
the whole engine.

14
00:00:37,063 --> 00:00:39,763
The problem is, nothing built into that loop guarantees it ever stops.

15
00:00:40,063 --> 00:00:42,497
Say you've got a coding agent trying to fix a failing test.

16
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:45,163
It runs the test, sees it fail, edits the code, runs the

17
00:00:45,163 --> 00:00:45,963
test again.

18
00:00:46,097 --> 00:00:49,030
Reasonable so far. But if its fix doesn't actually address the real

19
00:00:49,030 --> 00:00:51,897
problem, the test fails again, in the exact same way. So it

20
00:00:51,897 --> 00:00:53,830
tries again. Same failure. Again.

21
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:57,197
The agent has no built-in sense of "I've tried this three times,

22
00:00:57,397 --> 00:00:58,997
something's fundamentally wrong here."

23
00:00:59,630 --> 00:01:02,963
It just keeps observing the same failure, and reacting the same way,

24
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:05,730
forever — unless something outside the loop stops it.

25
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:09,297
Every AI model has something like a working memory — a limited

26
00:01:09,297 --> 00:01:12,063
window of text it can actually pay attention to at once.

27
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:15,330
As a task runs longer, more and more gets stuffed into that

28
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:19,563
window: the original instructions, ten tool results, error messages, retries, side

29
00:01:19,563 --> 00:01:20,363
conversations.

30
00:01:20,797 --> 00:01:23,430
Eventually, older information starts getting crowded out, or just

31
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:27,230
deprioritized. The agent's original goal — the thing you actually asked for

32
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:28,763
— was three hundred messages ago.

33
00:01:29,397 --> 00:01:32,363
What's left in its immediate attention is the last few tool calls

34
00:01:32,363 --> 00:01:33,163
and errors.

35
00:01:33,297 --> 00:01:36,663
So it starts optimizing for "make this error go away," instead of

36
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:38,597
"accomplish what the user actually wanted."

37
00:01:39,097 --> 00:01:42,863
That's context drift. The agent hasn't forgotten in a dramatic way —

38
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:44,897
it's just lost the thread of what mattered most.

39
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:48,183
Every tool an agent can use has a strict shape it expects

40
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:50,283
— specific fields, specific formats.

41
00:01:50,717 --> 00:01:53,417
Like a form that only accepts a date in one exact format,

42
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:56,483
or a function that only accepts a real, existing file name.

43
00:01:56,917 --> 00:02:00,383
Sometimes the model predicts an argument that sounds exactly right — plausible,

44
00:02:00,917 --> 00:02:02,883
well-formatted, confident — but it's just made up.

45
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:06,183
Maybe it's referencing a file that was deleted three steps ago.

46
00:02:06,783 --> 00:02:09,250
Maybe it's inventing a setting the tool never supported.

47
00:02:09,617 --> 00:02:13,083
The tool call either throws an error, or worse, silently does something

48
00:02:13,083 --> 00:02:14,583
slightly different than intended.

49
00:02:15,017 --> 00:02:17,450
And because language models are built to produce fluent,

50
00:02:17,917 --> 00:02:21,883
confident-sounding text, a hallucinated argument doesn't look like a guess. It looks

51
00:02:21,883 --> 00:02:23,083
exactly like a fact.

52
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:26,583
This is the failure mode that makes the other three dangerous, instead

53
00:02:26,583 --> 00:02:27,383
of just annoying.

54
00:02:27,717 --> 00:02:30,283
An agent has no built-in alarm bell for "I am stuck."

55
00:02:30,717 --> 00:02:34,217
It doesn't experience frustration or doubt. So after ten failed attempts at

56
00:02:34,217 --> 00:02:37,117
the same fix, it can still generate a final summary that reads:

57
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:38,783
"Task completed successfully."

58
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:42,317
That gap — between what actually happened, and what the agent reports

59
00:02:42,317 --> 00:02:45,583
happened — is the single most dangerous thing about agent failures.

60
00:02:46,217 --> 00:02:48,383
A human who's stuck usually looks stuck.

61
00:02:48,817 --> 00:02:52,050
An agent that's stuck can look identical to an agent that succeeded,

62
00:02:52,417 --> 00:02:54,783
right up until someone checks the actual result.

63
00:02:55,470 --> 00:02:58,303
Let's walk through one, start to finish. You ask a coding agent

64
00:02:58,303 --> 00:03:01,103
to deploy a small website update. Step one — it runs the

65
00:03:01,103 --> 00:03:04,103
deploy command. Step two — the deploy fails, because of a missing

66
00:03:04,103 --> 00:03:05,070
environment variable.

67
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:07,737
Step three — instead of asking you what that variable should be,

68
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:10,037
it guesses a value, and hallucinates it into the config.

69
00:03:10,503 --> 00:03:13,437
Step four — the deploy runs again, fails differently now, because the

70
00:03:13,537 --> 00:03:14,337
guessed value was wrong.

71
00:03:14,803 --> 00:03:16,703
Step five — the agent, still in the loop,

72
00:03:17,137 --> 00:03:20,537
tries a slightly different guess. Step six — same failure, different guess.

73
00:03:20,737 --> 00:03:24,237
This repeats twelve times. By attempt twelve, the context window is packed

74
00:03:24,237 --> 00:03:28,237
with failed deploy logs. The original instruction — "deploy this small update"

75
00:03:28,303 --> 00:03:30,637
— has been buried under eleven rounds of error messages.

76
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:34,237
And the agent, unprompted, hasn't once said, "I don't actually know this

77
00:03:34,237 --> 00:03:35,337
value. Can you tell me?"

78
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,593
So how do you actually stop this? Three guardrails — and they

79
00:03:38,593 --> 00:03:39,960
work together. A turn limit.

80
00:03:40,393 --> 00:03:43,027
Cap how many loop cycles the agent gets, before it's forced to

81
00:03:43,027 --> 00:03:45,493
stop and report status instead of continuing silently.

82
00:03:45,927 --> 00:03:49,827
Twelve failed deploy attempts becomes three, then a forced stop. A verifier

83
00:03:49,827 --> 00:03:50,627
step.

84
00:03:50,593 --> 00:03:53,593
Before the agent marks anything as done, a separate check — sometimes

85
00:03:53,593 --> 00:03:57,460
a second model call, sometimes a hard rule — actually confirms the

86
00:03:57,693 --> 00:03:59,093
real-world result matches the claim.

87
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:02,627
Did the website actually go live? Check. Don't just ask the agent

88
00:04:02,627 --> 00:04:05,793
if it thinks it did. A human-in-the-loop gate. For anything with real

89
00:04:05,793 --> 00:04:09,227
consequences — spending money, deleting a file, deploying to production — the

90
00:04:09,227 --> 00:04:12,060
agent proposes the action and stops, and a person has to approve

91
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:13,193
it before it executes.

92
00:04:13,793 --> 00:04:17,527
This turns "confidently wrong" from a silent disaster, into a visible pause

93
00:04:17,527 --> 00:04:18,327
you can catch.

94
00:04:18,930 --> 00:04:20,763
None of these fixes make an agent smarter.

95
00:04:21,163 --> 00:04:23,797
They just make its failures visible, and its damage limited — which,

96
00:04:23,797 --> 00:04:26,697
it turns out, is most of the actual engineering work behind building

97
00:04:26,697 --> 00:04:28,630
agents that don't quietly wreck your afternoon.

98
00:04:29,097 --> 00:04:31,963
So your turn. Here's the prompt: "Take an agent workflow I rely

99
00:04:31,963 --> 00:04:33,830
on and find where it can fail silently."

100
00:04:34,463 --> 00:04:37,163
Run it against something that touches money, files, or a deploy.

101
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:39,897
Then check the answer for three things — does it name a

102
00:04:39,897 --> 00:04:40,697
turn limit,

103
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:43,663
does it name a verifier that checks the real result, and does

104
00:04:43,663 --> 00:04:45,797
it name a human gate on the irreversible step?

105
00:04:46,163 --> 00:04:48,830
If it comes back saying "write a better prompt" and stops there,

106
00:04:48,997 --> 00:04:50,197
it missed the point entirely.

107
00:04:50,897 --> 00:04:53,497
Next time an agent tells you it's done, ask yourself — did

108
00:04:53,497 --> 00:04:54,630
anything actually check that?

109
00:04:54,997 --> 00:04:57,930
If the answer's no, you're trusting a system that's never once had

110
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:00,763
to know the difference between succeeding, and just saying so.

111
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:02,200
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
